<p>Norfolk is known for its network of rivers and lakes, its stock of medieval churches, and for being <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eTyl3o6N_14">the&nbsp;home county of Alan Partridge</a>. Last year, it quite literally unearthed another mark of distinction in the form of an iron age war trumpet.&nbsp;“The bronze trumpet or carnyx is only the third ever found in Britain, and the most complete example discovered anywhere in the world,” <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/jan/07/iron-age-war-trumpet-find-britain-norfolk-boudicca-links">writes the&nbsp;<em>Guardian</em>’s Esther Addley</a>. “Fashioned in the shape of a snarling wild animal, the object would have been mounted on a long mouthpiece high above the heads of warriors, allowing it to be sounded to intimidate the enemy in battle.” One could hardly hope for a more powerful symbol of Celtic fearsomeness.</p>
<p>Yet “there are many contradictions” in the longer story of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnyx">carnyx</a>, says environmental scientist Paul Whitewick in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=68Hds0OYrbg">his new video</a>, “from where it’s been found to its intended use, its depiction on art and coins: none of those seem to make sense.” The most famous head of a carnyx was uncovered in a Scottish peat bog; another came out of the River Witham in Lincolnshire.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>In addition to those recoveries from non-battlefield settings, one detailed ironwork panel commonly referenced in carnyx reconstruction presents its use in more of a procession. It also became a symbol on Roman-era coinage, and with an apparently wide range of meaning. Perhaps the carnyx was a war horn; perhaps it was much more.</p>
