<p>Wherever the ancients typically broke them out, carnyces aren’t for discussing, but for hearing. None was sounded for two millennia, until the reconstruction of the one found in the peat bog, known as the Deskford Carnyx. The first musician to give it a blow, in 1993, was John Kenny, who’s been known as the world’s foremost carnyx player ever since. He does so in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lVFGT2NX-YQ">the video just above</a> from National Museums Scotland, which offers not just the instrument’s haunting sound, but a close-up view of its wagging tongue. Kenny, too, believes that carnyces have uses beyond war, and even that “they are more impressive when they are played less loudly.” Still, just imagine how intimidated an enemy army would feel upon hearing a whole line of them sounded at once — to say nothing of seeing all those tongues.</p>
